<details><summary>More findings: manual and startup capture</summary>

### Manual still promises image history

`manual/08-unified-clipboard-history.md` line 18 ("works for both text and images") and `manual/03-coming-from-mac-or-windows.md` line 27 ("it holds images as well as text") are unchanged. **Impact:** users are told new image copies appear in history when they no longer do.

**Suggested change:** Update both lines if image history is removed.

### Image capture is only partly removed

The shell-start snapshot (`currentProc`, which runs `capture.sh` with no arguments) still tries image types first (`capture.sh` lines 97-102). When the clipboard holds an image, each shell start therefore still reads and stores it, as the last line of the first reproducer shows. **Impact:** image capture continues in one place, and the `image/*` branch of `capture.sh` and its tests now cover a path no watcher invokes.

**Suggested change:** Either keep image capture or remove it from the startup snapshot too, so the behaviour is consistent.
</details>
<details><summary>Details</summary>

**Overlap with open PRs:** #11920 conflicts in `watchRestartTimer` and adds more `imageWatchProc` handling (a merge leaves references to the deleted process). #7452 (OCR and an images-only view) depends on image capture. #9488 bounds the stalled-callback case and merges cleanly with this PR.

**Hypothesis (simulated, not the reporter's confirmed cause):** `wl-paste --watch` waits for each callback, and `capture.sh` runs an unbounded `wl-paste --list-types` (line 13) before reading its stdin. `wl-copy` serves one read at a time. If a callback stalls before draining a payload above the 64 KiB pipe buffer, other pastes wait until it drains (in the run below, nothing arrived within 3 s) and a replaced `wl-copy` lingers. This affects large text as well as images (issue #9443 reports a stuck `capture.sh text`).

This is not a reproduction of the reporter's hang. It shows a mechanism that produces the symptoms the PR describes ("leaves the user's paste target with nothing and results in stuck `wl-copy` processes").

`wl-paste --watch` hands the payload to `capture.sh` through a pipe. `capture.sh` runs `wl-paste --list-types` (line 13) before it reads that pipe. `wl-copy` serves one read at a time and waits until each reader has drained it (`do_send`, `src/types/copy-action.c`, lines 89-123, wl-clipboard 2.3.0). In this script a wrapper makes that `--list-types` call sleep 6 s, as a stand-in for a callback that stops before draining its pipe (issue #9443 reports a `capture.sh text` callback stuck for two days).

With a 3 MiB payload, a paste during the stall gets nothing within its 3 s timeout, and the old `wl-copy` keeps running after a new copy replaces it until the stall ends. This happens with the text watcher the PR keeps, not only with the image watcher. A 16 KiB payload fits in the 64 KiB pipe buffer and is not affected.

Run: put `relay.c` (from the first reproducer) and `repro-stall.sh` in one directory, then `OMARCHY=/path/to/omarchy-checkout bash repro-stall.sh WATCHER-TYPE MIME BYTES`.

## repro-stall.sh

```bash
#!/bin/bash
# What happens to wl-copy when a watcher callback stops before reading its pipe?
# Simulation: a wrapper makes `wl-paste --list-types` (capture.sh runs it before
# reading stdin) sleep 6 s. Usage: OMARCHY=... bash repro-stall.sh WATCHER-TYPE MIME BYTES
#   e.g. image/png image/png 3145728   (watcher removed by the PR)
#        text text/plain 3145728        (watcher kept by the PR)
#        text text/plain 16384
set -u
watch_type=$1 mime=$2 bytes=$3
here=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)
work=$(mktemp -d)
pids=()
trap 'kill "${pids[@]}" 2>/dev/null; wait 2>/dev/null; rm -rf "$work"' EXIT
export XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=$work HOME=$work/home XDG_STATE_HOME=$work/state
mkdir -p "$HOME" "$XDG_STATE_HOME" "$work/bin"

xml=$(pkg-config --variable=pkgdatadir wayland-protocols)/staging/ext-data-control/ext-data-control-v1.xml
wayland-scanner server-header "$xml" "$work/ext-data-control-v1-server-protocol.h"
wayland-scanner private-code "$xml" "$work/ext-data-control-v1-protocol.c"
gcc -I"$work" -o "$work/relay" "$here/relay.c" "$work/ext-data-control-v1-protocol.c" -lwayland-server || exit 1
"$work/relay" >"$work/display" 2>/dev/null &
pids+=($!)
until [[ -s $work/display ]]; do sleep 0.05; done
export WAYLAND_DISPLAY=$(<"$work/display")

real=$(command -v wl-paste)
cat >"$work/bin/wl-paste" <<SH
#!/bin/bash
[[ \${1:-} == --list-types && -e $work/stall ]] && sleep 6
exec $real "\$@"
SH
chmod +x "$work/bin/wl-paste"

capture=$OMARCHY/shell/plugins/clipboard/capture.sh
PATH="$work/bin:$PATH" setpriv --pdeathsig TERM wl-paste --type "$watch_type" --watch "$capture" "$watch_type" >"$work/watcher.out" &
pids+=($!)
sleep 1

if [[ $mime == image/* ]]; then head -c "$bytes" /dev/urandom >"$work/payload"; else head -c "$bytes" /dev/zero | tr '\0' a >"$work/payload"; fi
touch "$work/stall"
wl-copy --foreground --type "$mime" <"$work/payload" &
old=$!
pids+=($old)
